## =============================================================================
## Manual extraction + review walkthrough (2026-09-02)
## =============================================================================
## Matt: "setup some manual testing of extraction and review process for me."
##
## Every other story here documents an AUTOMATED run. This one is written to be
## driven BY HAND, because the two things it exercises cannot be asserted
## headlessly: whether extraction produced the right SHAPE, and whether the
## review queue is something a person can actually get through. Both are
## judgements, and the offline chain harness measures around them rather than
## through them.
##
## Inputs are tests/fixtures/notes-corpus/ — raw TEXT, because every other
## fixture in this repo is already-extracted triples and therefore exercises the
## extractor not at all. Each input has a companion EXPECTED file naming what a
## correct run produces AND what is known-broken, so a run is judgeable rather
## than merely observable.
## =============================================================================

test:ManualExtractionReview
    rdf:type            story:Story ;
    rdfs:label          "Manual extraction & review walkthrough" ;
    story:description   "Drive extraction and review by hand against a raw-text corpus, smallest input first, judging SHAPE rather than counting triples. Written because the two questions that matter here — did extraction produce the right shape, and can a person get through the queue — are judgements no headless assertion reaches." ;
    story:autoplay      "false" ;
    story:pace          "0" ;
    skos:broader        test:GraphReviewStory .

test:ManualER-Step0
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Set the backend, and know which one you set" ;
    story:content       "Run `npm run dev` (port 5173), open /settings, set the ingest backend to ollama with base URL http://localhost:11434. DO NOT use `npm run dev:test` for this walkthrough: it forces the mock extractor, which yields only judgment-altitude facts and therefore produces no cascadable cluster at all — a mock run cannot exercise aggregation and will look misleadingly clean. Confirm Ollama is up first: curl -s localhost:11434/api/tags." ;
    kpred:assertion     "settings show ingest backend = ollama, and /api/tags responds" ;
    kpred:page          "/settings" .

test:ManualER-Step1
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Start on a clean graph, or you cannot read the result" ;
    story:content       "Create a NEW empty graph to extract into. Two test graphs are already synced in the workspace and neither is the target: `extraction-corpus` holds the raw text to copy FROM, and `extraction-tests` holds already-extracted triples for exercising the analysis chain without running the extractor at all. Extract into a fresh graph instead — vocabulary repair matches against names the graph ALREADY HAS, so a dirty start makes repair proposals unattributable to the note that triggered them, and mixing input with output means you can no longer tell which facts came from where." ;
    kpred:assertion     "graph is empty: 0 statements, 0 entities" ;
    kpred:page          "/" .

test:ManualER-Step2
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Ingest one sentence pair — 01-note-single.txt" ;
    story:content       "Open the `extraction-corpus` graph in the workspace and select kb:note-01-single. Copy its kpred:captured-note literal and ingest it into the FRESH graph from step 1 — never into extraction-corpus itself, which is the source, not the target. The ONLY question this file asks: are the two claims separated into distinct facts with a real subject, or collapsed into one sentence-shaped node? Read the node's kpred:expected first — three of the plausible complaints are known-broken and already recorded." ;
    kpred:assertion     "no entity slug contains a copula; 3-4 pending facts" ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" .

test:ManualER-Step3
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Ingest eight dictated notes — 02-notes-batch.txt" ;
    story:content       "kb:note-02-batch in the `extraction-corpus` graph — the one that matters: every damage pattern catalogued from the real private notes corpus, plus enough vocabulary for repair to have something to match. Seven things to judge, in its kpred:expected. The highest-value one is FIRST: the last two notes are REQUESTS, and must become tasks rather than facts. A fact asserting a document was generated is a claim nobody made. Ingest AFTER 01 — 01 seeds the vocabulary that makes repair possible here." ;
    kpred:assertion     "2 tasks routed, 0 facts asserting the requests" ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" .

test:ManualER-Step4
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Watch the seam: repair offers to destroy five siblings" ;
    story:content       "In /review, find the node-attribute family — name, value, type, count, and the repeat-count one. EXPECTED TODAY: vocabulary-repair offers to MERGE them, which would destroy four distinct attributes, and NOTHING proposes a parent. proposeCoHyponyms exists (src/lib/rdf/co-hyponyms.ts, 10 tests) and would withdraw those merges and propose one parent instead, but it is wired only into the offline harness. Confirming the bad merges appear IS the test — this step exists to show the seam in the UI rather than in a report." ;
    kpred:assertion     "merge proposals present between node-attribute siblings; no parent proposed" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" .

test:ManualER-Step4b
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Cross-check against the triples-only graph" ;
    story:content       "Open `extraction-tests` in the workspace — 41 statements already extracted, so it exercises the analysis chain with the extractor held constant. Its blocks are labelled: mis-transcriptions repair SHOULD catch, note ids it must not merge, sentence-shaped slugs, the co-hyponym seam, and a working skos:broader hierarchy as the control. If the app behaves differently here than `npx tsx scripts/offline/extraction-chain.ts --graph=reckons-workspace/kbs/extraction-tests/extraction-tests.ttl`, the difference is in the app's wiring rather than in the rules — which is the most useful kind of finding this walkthrough can produce." ;
    kpred:assertion     "app and harness agree on the same graph, or the difference is recorded" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" .

test:ManualER-Step5
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "6"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Judge the queue as a person, not as a count" ;
    story:content       "The question is not how many facts are pending, it is whether you can get through them. Work the queue for five minutes and record: how many clicks to settle one CLAIM; whether bookkeeping stayed behind a count; whether any decision root appeared at all. Measured baseline on the real corpus (2026-09-02): 33 batch questions over 148 bookkeeping facts, and 53 claims loose under ZERO decisions. If that repeats here, the flat list is reproduced and the number to beat is 0." ;
    kpred:assertion     "record: clicks per settled claim, and decisions-open count" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" .

test:ManualER-Step6
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "7"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Ingest structured prose — 03-doc-medium.md" ;
    story:content       "kb:note-03-medium in the `extraction-corpus` graph. Where 02 tests damage, this tests STRUCTURE. Two things to watch above all: (1) do the opinions keep their author — 'we assess the terms as unfavourable' must not become a flat measurement; (2) does the 'What we do not know' section survive as QUESTIONS. Two absences are stated outright there, which is the rarest thing a document can contain (kb:mission). If they are dropped, the extractor cannot represent missing information at all — and that is the most important single result in this walkthrough." ;
    kpred:assertion     "the 2 stated unknowns appear as questions or partial facts" ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" .

test:ManualER-Step7
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "8"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Let the two sources disagree" ;
    story:content       "03 says 'enterprise DAM' where 02 said 'enterprise cad' and 'enterprise damn'. A second source disagreeing with the first is precisely what the review queue exists for. Confirm the disagreement SURFACES as something to settle rather than the later ingest silently overwriting the earlier. Silent overwrite is the worst outcome available here and would be a finding worth stopping for." ;
    kpred:assertion     "disagreement is visible; no silent overwrite" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" .

test:ManualER-Step8
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "9"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Only now, go big — 04-article-large.md" ;
    story:content       "kb:note-04-large carries the fetch instructions; the text itself is deliberately not committed. Fetch a real article and ingest it LAST. Judge SHAPE, not accuracy: chunk seams (a subject named in one chunk, pronoun in the next), whether review survives several hundred entities, the false-positive rate of repair at volume, and whether the UI stays honest about progress. Accuracy at this size is what F146 phase 1's scored corpus is for, and that corpus does not exist yet — so do not attempt to grade triples by reading them." ;
    kpred:assertion     "record: chunk-seam orphans, queue survivability, drift rate, wall time" ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" .

test:ManualER-Step9
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ManualExtractionReview ;
    story:order         "10"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Put findings back in the graph, not in chat" ;
    story:content       "Every observation goes to the review queue as a pending entry — kb_add_note, or a JSONL line in reckons-workspace/knowledge.pending.jsonl — never as a chat report that evaporates. Re-run `npx tsx scripts/offline/extraction-chain.ts --graph=<the graph you just built>` and compare against the 2026-09-02 baseline recorded on kb:extraction-scoring: the harness measures the same graph you just judged by hand, and the two readings disagreeing is itself information." ;
    kpred:assertion     "findings queued; chain harness re-run and compared to baseline" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" .


## =============================================================================
## Persona
## =============================================================================

@prefix shelly: <urn:reckons:shelly/> .

## =============================================================================
## Review workload after enhanced extraction (2026-09-04)
## =============================================================================
## Matt: "we have attempted the summary, hierarchy, and other elements to reduce
## reviewer workload, but now with enhanced extraction we should test again."
##
## The graph under test is GENERATED, not hand-written:
##   npx tsx scripts/offline/build-review-corpus.ts
## which runs raw note text through the real pipeline — ungrounded extraction with
## qwen3:32b, passage-verified excerpts, then deterministic vocabulary reconcile —
## and writes every statement PENDING so it arrives as review work.
##
## Read the measured result below before you start. The automated chain already
## says the decision tree is EMPTY. This walkthrough is not asking you to discover
## that; it is asking whether the flat list is nonetheless navigable, and which of
## the three missing mechanisms you would want first.
## =============================================================================

test:ReviewWorkloadAfterExtraction
    rdf:type            story:Story ;
    rdfs:label          "Review workload after enhanced extraction" ;
    story:description   "Re-test whether summary, hierarchy and aggregation reduce reviewer workload, now that extraction runs on qwen3:32b with grounding deferred to post-hoc reconciliation. The automated half is already measured and negative — the tree is still empty. What a person has to judge is whether the queue is WORKABLE anyway, and what is actually missing." ;
    story:autoplay      "false" ;
    story:pace          "0" ;
    kpred:measured      "2026-09-04, generated corpus (3 sources, 41 statements, all pending). IMPROVED vs the 2026-09-02 baseline: 0 sentence-shaped entity slugs, down from 3 — triple-shape.ts is holding and the collapsed-subject failure is gone. UNCHANGED: 0 types proposed (0 of 11 untyped settleable), 0 entities placed in a hierarchy, 39 orphans, 0 decisions open, 32 loose judgments. Aggregation now produces NOTHING at all, because 41 statements across 3 sources contains no bookkeeping cluster big enough to be worth one question — on the 217-statement baseline it formed 33. So better extraction did not reduce review workload: it produced cleaner facts in the same flat list." ;
    skos:broader        test:GraphReviewStory .

test:RWAE-Step0
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ReviewWorkloadAfterExtraction ;
    story:order         "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Generate the corpus yourself, or trust the committed one" ;
    story:content       "Extraction is nondeterministic — three runs of the same corpus produced 34, 40 and 41 statements — so regenerate only if you want to see that variance, and expect the numbers below to shift by a few. Run: OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434 npx tsx scripts/offline/build-review-corpus.ts. It writes reckons-workspace/kbs/review-test/review-test.ttl and mutates nothing else. Then run npx tsx scripts/offline/extraction-chain.ts --graph=reckons-workspace/kbs/review-test/review-test.ttl to see the machine's verdict before you form your own." ;
    kpred:assertion     "review-test.ttl exists and the chain reports a non-zero statement count" .

test:RWAE-Step1
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ReviewWorkloadAfterExtraction ;
    story:order         "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Open the review-test graph and go to /review" ;
    story:content       "Run `npm run dev` (port 5173) — NOT dev:test, which forces the mock extractor. Switch to the review-test graph. Every statement is pending on purpose, so the queue should be full immediately. If the queue is EMPTY, the graph imported as confirmed and something in the annotated-Turtle round-trip broke; that is a bug, not a clean run." ;
    kpred:assertion     "the review queue shows roughly 40 pending facts, not zero" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" .

test:RWAE-Step2
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ReviewWorkloadAfterExtraction ;
    story:order         "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "The main question — how many decisions does it ask you to make?" ;
    story:content       "Count what the page actually puts in front of you. The chain harness says 0 decisions and 32 loose judgments, which means a flat list. Judge whether that is true in the UI, and then judge the thing no harness can: could you get through it? Time yourself on the first ten. If you find yourself re-reading the same entity under two names, note which two — that is the reconcile gap, not a review-UI gap." ;
    kpred:assertion     "you can state a number: how many distinct decisions the queue asked for" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" .

test:RWAE-Step3
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ReviewWorkloadAfterExtraction ;
    story:order         "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Check the five summaries — do any say what the facts CLAIM?" ;
    story:content       "reviewTreeSummary, reviewPlanSummary, attentionSummary, routingSummary and reanalysisSummary all count QUEUE SHAPE ('12 decisions open; 3 contested'). None says what the facts assert. That was the 2026-09-02 root cause of 'I have not seen success yet on a great summary of the pending facts to approve', and it is still unbuilt. Confirm it is still true rather than assuming — if a summary now describes CONTENT, that is new and worth recording." ;
    kpred:assertion     "you can say whether any on-screen summary describes content rather than counts" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" .

test:RWAE-Step4
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ReviewWorkloadAfterExtraction ;
    story:order         "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Find the reconcile proposals — and notice they are not here" ;
    story:content       "vocabulary-reconcile.ts produced suggest-band proposals during the build (e.g. `lumenpath ~ lumen-path`, phonetic 0.85, and across documents `jordan-vale ~ jordan-veil`). They were computed, logged to console, and then went nowhere: they have NO review surface yet. This is a KNOWN GAP recorded on kb:deferred-grounding, not a bug to file. What to judge: when you meet the two spellings as separate rows in the queue, how much work does settling them by hand cost? That cost is the argument for building the surface." ;
    kpred:assertion     "you can find both spellings of at least one name as separate queue rows" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" .

test:RWAE-Step5
    rdf:type            story:Step ;
    story:partOf        test:ReviewWorkloadAfterExtraction ;
    story:order         "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    story:title         "Decide which missing mechanism you want first" ;
    story:content       "Three things independently produce zero, and each is a different fix. TYPING proposes 0 of 11 because no built-in type carries the predicates a dictated note produces (F149's rdfs:domain gap). HIERARCHY places 0 because extraction emits no skos:broader at all, and proposeCoHyponyms is still offline-only. AGGREGATION forms 0 questions because a 41-statement graph has no bookkeeping cluster worth batching — it needs volume, which means this test may simply be too small to exercise it. Your call on the order; only the first two are within reach without more data." ;
    kpred:assertion     "a stated preference, recorded on kb:extraction-strategies" .

shelly:persona
    shelly:name             "Shelly" ;
    shelly:greeting         "This is the test documentation! I can walk you through any test story step-by-step, show you screenshots, or explain the test infrastructure. The Dev Sprint story is a great place to start!" ;
    shelly:personality      "methodical, visual, precise" ;
    shelly:responseStyle    "concise" ;
    shelly:systemPrompt     "You are helping someone understand the Reckons.AI test suite. Walk through stories in order, reference screenshots, and explain what each step verifies. The stories are ordered — follow the sequence." .


## =============================================================================
## Test workflows (F34.2)
## =============================================================================
## Each workflow is a ktype:TestWorkflow with ordered ktype:TestStep entities. The step list is read
## by ONE module (scripts/lib/test-workflow.ts) that feeds the generated flow diagram, the step
## sections of the page, the e2e test that runs the steps, and the screenshot check — so none of
## them can disagree with another. kpred:screenshot is written by `npm run workflow:shots`, never by
## hand; a step with no kpred:screenshot has no captured screenshot yet.

test:TestWorkflows
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Test workflows" ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "112"^^xsd:integer ;
    skos:definition     """A test workflow is one whole task a person does in Reckons.AI, written down as numbered steps and then run, step by step, by an automated test in a real browser. Each workflow page shows the steps as a diagram, says in plain words what happens at each step, shows a screenshot of the real app with a box drawn round the control being checked, and states the one thing the test checks at that step.

### Why a page for each

A passing test only says "nothing broke". A workflow page lets a person who has never opened the app see what the test actually does, in the order they would do it, and judge for themselves whether the check at each step is a meaningful one.

### How to read a workflow page

1. **The diagram** is drawn from the step list itself, so it always matches what the test runs.
2. **Each step** has the words for what happens, where it happens, the screenshot, then **What the test checks**.
3. **The pink box** in a screenshot marks the control or area that step is about.

### What is honest about this

Screenshots are taken on synthetic example data, never on anyone's real notes. A step whose screenshot has not been captured yet says so instead of showing a stand-in picture. The test confirms the app responds at every step; it does not judge how good the extracted claims or a reckoning's proposal are, which are measured separately. Each workflow page says which parts are scripted stand-ins for a model.""" ;
    skos:broader        test:TestSuite .

test:Workflow-ReadmePath
    rdf:type            ktype:TestWorkflow ;
    rdfs:label          "Add a source, review it, see it in a space, ask for a reckoning" ;
    skos:definition     """The path the README describes, start to finish: put a short note into Reckons.AI, settle the claims it proposes, find them in your space, and ask for a reckoning that works from what you settled. It is the whole point of the product in five steps, and a browser test runs it on every change.""" ;
    kpred:shots-note    """## How these screenshots were made

**What they show.**

Every step is run on the **Getting started** sample that a first-time visitor opens, in which two people, Alex and Jordan, plan a camping weekend. Everything in it is invented, so the claims, counts and names in the pictures are sample content and not anyone's real notes.

**What is scripted.**

The test uses no language model. When it adds its own note in step 1, a stand-in reader returns placeholder claims, so the claims you see waiting in steps 2 and 3 are the ones Getting started already holds. In step 5 the reckoning is a fixed reply, written in advance to read like a real one. So the pictures show what each screen looks like and that it responds. They do not show how good a real model's claims or proposal would be, which is measured separately.""" ;
    kpred:workflow-id   "readme-path" ;
    kpred:test-file     "tests/e2e/workflow-readme-path.test.ts" ;
    kpred:render-inline "true" ;
    kpred:children-label "The steps" ;
    skos:broader        test:TestWorkflows .

test:Workflow-ReadmePath-Step1
    rdf:type            ktype:TestStep ;
    rdfs:label          "Add a source" ;
    kpred:part-of       test:Workflow-ReadmePath ;
    kpred:step-order    "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:step-id       "add-source" ;
    kpred:short-label   "Add a source" ;
    kpred:box-label     "Extract facts" ;
    kpred:box-target    "the Extract facts button, under the note's title and text" ;
    kpred:page          "/ingest" ;
    kpred:page-name     "the Add tab" ;
    kpred:screenshot    "/workflows/readme-path/01-add-source.webp" ;
    skos:definition     "Open the Add tab, give the note a title, type or paste in a few sentences and press Extract facts. Reckons.AI reads the text and proposes claims from it. Nothing is accepted yet: every claim arrives waiting for your decision." ;
    kpred:assertion     "The Extract facts button is enabled once a title and text are entered, and pressing it takes the person off the Add tab when reading finishes. The test's stand-in reader returns placeholder claims at this point." .

test:Workflow-ReadmePath-Step2
    rdf:type            ktype:TestStep ;
    rdfs:label          "Review the proposed claims" ;
    kpred:part-of       test:Workflow-ReadmePath ;
    kpred:step-order    "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:step-id       "review-claims" ;
    kpred:short-label   "Review the claims" ;
    kpred:box-label     "A claim waiting" ;
    kpred:box-target    "one claim card: the claim, the passage it was taken from, and its accept, refine and reject buttons" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" ;
    kpred:page-name     "the Review tab" ;
    kpred:screenshot    "/workflows/readme-path/02-review-claims.webp" ;
    skos:definition     "Open Review. Each claim waiting for a decision is a card. It names where the claim came from, shows the passage it was taken from, and offers accept, refine and reject. AI can misread what it reads, so this is where you check what it proposed against the original text." ;
    kpred:assertion     "At least one claim is waiting on the Review tab, shown as a card with accept, refine and reject buttons." .

test:Workflow-ReadmePath-Step3
    rdf:type            ktype:TestStep ;
    rdfs:label          "Confirm the claims that are right" ;
    kpred:part-of       test:Workflow-ReadmePath ;
    kpred:step-order    "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:step-id       "confirm-claims" ;
    kpred:short-label   "Confirm what is right" ;
    kpred:box-label     "Accept" ;
    kpred:box-target    "the accept button on the first claim card" ;
    kpred:page          "/review" ;
    kpred:page-name     "the Review tab" ;
    kpred:screenshot    "/workflows/readme-path/03-confirm-claims.webp" ;
    skos:definition     "Press accept on each claim that matches its source. You can also refine a claim or reject it. Only claims you confirm become part of the space; anything left waiting stays out." ;
    kpred:assertion     "Pressing accept settles the claims one by one until none of them is left waiting for a decision." .

test:Workflow-ReadmePath-Step4
    rdf:type            ktype:TestStep ;
    rdfs:label          "See the claims in your space" ;
    kpred:part-of       test:Workflow-ReadmePath ;
    kpred:step-order    "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:step-id       "see-in-space" ;
    kpred:short-label   "See it in your space" ;
    kpred:box-label     "Your space" ;
    kpred:box-target    "the card marked current, which shows its name and how many facts it holds" ;
    kpred:page          "/kb" ;
    kpred:page-name     "the Spaces tab" ;
    kpred:screenshot    "/workflows/readme-path/04-see-in-space.webp" ;
    skos:definition     "Open Spaces. The claims you confirmed are now facts in your space, the collection of connected knowledge that everything you add feeds. The one you are working in is marked current, and its card says how many facts it holds." ;
    kpred:assertion     "The Spaces tab shows the current one with more facts than it held before the claims were confirmed." .

test:Workflow-ReadmePath-Step5
    rdf:type            ktype:TestStep ;
    rdfs:label          "Ask for a reckoning" ;
    kpred:part-of       test:Workflow-ReadmePath ;
    kpred:step-order    "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    kpred:step-id       "reckoning" ;
    kpred:short-label   "Ask for a reckoning" ;
    kpred:box-label     "Proposal" ;
    kpred:box-target    "the proposal card, which says how many facts it is based on" ;
    kpred:page          "/reckoning" ;
    kpred:page-name     "the Reckon tab" ;
    kpred:screenshot    "/workflows/readme-path/05-reckoning.webp" ;
    skos:definition     "Describe the situation you are in, say what you want to reach, and ask for a reckoning. It reads the facts in your space and proposes options and a recommendation, which you then weigh yourself. It is a proposal to evaluate, not an answer to trust." ;
    kpred:assertion     "The request is accepted and a proposal card comes back saying how many facts it is based on. The reply in this run is scripted, written to read like a real reckoning about the Getting started notes; the test does not judge a real model's wording, which is measured separately." .

test:Workflow-GeneratedAssets
    rdf:type            ktype:TestWorkflow ;
    rdfs:label          "Make pictures, clips and 3D splats on your own machine, and see them in a space" ;
    skos:definition     """Write a prompt, and three local models turn it into a picture, then a short clip and a Gaussian splat (a 3D object drawn as many small soft blobs of color) made from that picture. You can turn a splat and look at it from any side. The results open as their own space, where each one sits on its own node, linked to what it was made from, and one splat can become the background of the space.

### Made on one machine, with no cloud service

| Step | Tool | Model | License | What it made |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Picture from a prompt | stable-diffusion.cpp | Z-Image-Turbo | MIT and Apache-2.0 | a 768×768 picture |
| Clip from the picture | stable-diffusion.cpp | Wan2.2 TI2V-5B | MIT and Apache-2.0 | a 3-second, 49-frame clip |
| Splat from the picture | TripoSplat | TripoSplat | MIT | 32,768 or 65,536 Gaussians |

All three run on your own computer. The command is `npm run gen:assets`, which reads made-up prompts from `scripts/content/gen-briefs.json`. Each result gets a receipt beside it that names the tool, every model file with its fingerprint (a SHA-256 hash) and license, the prompt, the seed, how long it took, and whether the result passed its check. Tools and model files live outside the project folder; `scripts/content/gen-setup.sh` downloads them.

### A result is accepted by a check, not by the tool

A model can write a grey square, a clip in which nothing moves, or a splat full of broken numbers, and still report success. So every result is checked by code that knows nothing about the model. The picture must not be flat. The clip must play, and how much it moves is measured. The splat must have the expected number of blobs, real numbers, a real size and visible blobs. On the first run the splat check found a fault in TripoSplat's own file writer: thousands of blobs were saved with an infinite opacity. The pipeline now repairs those values and records how many it repaired.

### A generated splat is invented

TripoSplat guesses the far side of an object from one picture. A splat made this way is decoration, not a record of anything real, and the space says so. A splat rebuilt from many real photographs is a different thing and is not part of this workflow.""" ;
    kpred:shots-note    """## How these screenshots were made

**What they show.** The pictures, clips and splats are the committed results of one run of `npm run gen:assets` on one machine with an NVIDIA RTX 3090 graphics card, on 2026-10-14. Every prompt is made up for this example.

**What is not run here.** The browser test cannot run the models, because the machines that run the tests have no graphics card. For each result it checks that the file the app shows is byte for byte the one its receipt describes, that its check accepted it when it was made, and that the app shows it. Whether the models still produce good results is checked only when someone runs `npm run gen:assets` again.""" ;
    kpred:workflow-id   "generated-assets" ;
    kpred:test-file     "tests/e2e/workflow-generated-assets.test.ts" ;
    kpred:render-inline "true" ;
    kpred:children-label "The steps" ;
    skos:broader        test:TestWorkflows .

